event:federal_register:2026-17726::break.x:
The Department of Labor just withdrew the regulations that set formal procedures for coordinating enforcement across its farmworker programs.

The rule is final. It takes effect Sept. 30, 2026; until then the current procedures stay in force.

{deepLink}

event:uscis_policy_manual:20260831-voter.x:
USCIS just reversed its policy on voter registration at naturalization ceremonies.

What changed: the Aug. 29, 2025 policy is withdrawn and the earlier guidance is back, so nonpartisan nonprofits may again register new citizens right after the oath.

{deepLink}

event:uscis_newsroom:0cf4fd4e-c295-4784-.x:
A federal court has enjoined two USCIS memos on holds, PM 602-0192 and PM 602-0194, by order of Aug. 24, 2026.

Why it matters: the order stops enforcement as the court specifies. It does not rewrite the memos, and an appeal can stay or reverse it.

{deepLink}

event:federal_register:2026-14539::effec.x:
Starting Sept. 18, 2026, the 2022 public charge regulations are rescinded.

Until that date the 2022 rule stays in force. DHS published the final rule on July 20; the 2022 standard applies to determinations until the rescission takes effect.

{deepLink}

explainer:proposed-rule-vs-final-rule::e.x:
A proposed rule is not a rule.

A proposal invites comment, commonly for 30 to 60 days, and changes nothing by itself. A final rule is the agency's decision: it states an effective date, and it can differ from the proposal.

{deepLink}

